    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Female genital mutilation (FGM), a dangerous and dehumanising practice forced upon young girls, affects survivors for their entire lives through recurring physical and psychological issues, has been largely ignored and stigmatized. There is a fear of conversation surrounding this growing women's issue, which is ultimately not only a concern to girls and women in affected communities but also to men, and to the global community. The lack of discussion creates a barrier towards ending this cultural practice that is no longer confined to African countries. I argue that current efforts fall short of exposing this violation of human rights and that we all must overcome our fear in order to remove the stigma so we can begin to make progress against female genital mutilation.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
